WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.690
A new pupil joined my class in March.

2
00:00:03.430 --> 00:00:05.730
I had her reading speed and her

3
00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:07.550
accuracy from a short test.

4
00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:09.960
Which reading group should she join?

5
00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:14.690
Today: how a model can sort new cases by

6
00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:16.550
looking at the closest old ones.

7
00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:20.390
Regression predicts a number, like a mark.

8
00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:24.030
Classification predicts a label, like a

9
00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:26.050
reading group or a type of fruit.

10
00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:28.400
The model learns from examples

11
00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:30.280
that already have labels.

12
00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:34.720
Put every pupil on a scatter plot, speed

13
00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:37.960
across and accuracy up, coloured by group.

14
00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:40.090
Put the new pupil on it too.

15
00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:43.130
The nearest neighbour method gives her

16
00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:44.830
the group of the closest pupils.

17
00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:47.940
Here are her five nearest

18
00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:49.280
classmates by group.

19
00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:52.290
Three are in the blue group, and

20
00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:54.430
one each in red and green.

21
00:00:55.130 --> 00:00:56.070
The vote says blue.

22
00:00:57.310 --> 00:00:59.690
Closest means the shortest

23
00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:00.990
distance on the chart.

24
00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:04.320
We find it with Pythagoras: the square

25
00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:07.340
root of the gap in speed squared plus

26
00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:09.260
the gap in accuracy squared.

27
00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:11.500
Make the two measures use

28
00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:12.960
similar scales first.

29
00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:15.400
The number of neighbours

30
00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:16.820
we ask is called k.

31
00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:20.270
With k equal to one, a single

32
00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:21.590
odd pupil can decide.

33
00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:25.230
A small odd number such as three or

34
00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:28.090
five is a good start, and the test

35
00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:29.750
set tells you which is best.

36
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:31.480
Quick check!

37
00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:35.140
With k equal to three, two neighbours are

38
00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:37.800
in group A and one is in group B.

39
00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:40.380
Which group does the model choose?

40
00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:43.860
Group A, by two votes to one.

41
00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:47.310
The model simply copies the most common

42
00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:49.350
label among the nearest examples.

43
00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:51.860
That is why it is easy to

44
00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:52.880
explain to pupils.

45
00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:55.840
In the app, open the

46
00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:57.080
nearest neighbours lab.

47
00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:01.120
Drop a new point, change k, and watch the

48
00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:03.620
regions of each colour grow and shrink.

49
00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:05.460
Your turn.

50
00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:07.670
A model chooses a reading

51
00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:09.130
group for a new pupil.

52
00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:13.280
Does it predict a number or a label?

53
00:02:14.570 --> 00:02:17.030
A label, so it is classification.

54
00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:21.090
Remember: place the new case, find the

55
00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:23.630
k nearest examples by distance, and

56
00:02:23.630 --> 00:02:25.410
take the most common label.

57
00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:29.430
Try it yourself: weigh some fruit at home

58
00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:32.570
and score their colour from one to five.

59
00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:35.200
Then find the three nearest

60
00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:36.980
neighbours of a mystery fruit.
